     :emergent "eng: a TOTAL version of cdaar -- the same route, taken one step at a time, with a named sentinel where the shape runs out. math: the route as data, checked against the verb, which is the differential test written as a function. cs: one walker serves the whole family, because the route is an argument rather than part of the name."
     :emergent-name "the path walker"
     :meta "The composition is car and cdr plus an explicit pair? test plus the route as a list. What emerges is the accessor-as-data family: cdaar is one frozen instance of walk, and there are twenty-eight of them in R7RS-small. Freezing the route buys brevity and keeps the error, because the frozen instance raises where the walk would have run out of shape."
    )
    (:eng "When you want a reach that ANSWERS rather than raises, wrap it once with a sentinel rather than guarding at every call site."
     :math "Turning an index into an argument turns twenty-eight functions into one function of two arguments."
     :cs "A raise and a sentinel you chose are both honest; a value you cannot print is not. 'no-such-path prints, compares and lets the caller carry on."))
)

(verb
  :name "cdadr" :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(cdadr pair) -> object"
  :summary "cdadr: take the second item, then drop the first 1. Reads as cdr then car then cdr, and answers a LIST (a tail)."
  :explanation "One of the twenty-eight composed accessors of R7RS-small 6.4.1. Decode the name by dropping the leading c and the trailing r, then reading the 3 letters RIGHT TO LEFT: dad gives cdr then car then cdr -- take the second item, then drop the first 1. Written out it is (cdr (car (cdr pair))). The leading letter d tells you the answer is a LIST (a tail): a name whose leftmost letter is a answers an element, a name whose leftmost letter is d answers the rest of a list. You meet it on a table stored as a list of rows, where the job is dropping the label from the second row and keeping its data. On a mis-shaped input cdadr RAISES, and the message names cdadr itself: (cdadr '()) reports that it expected a pair or a non-empty list and got the empty list. R7RS-small 6.4.1 defines it as (cdr (car (cdr pair))), and that expansion raises in the same places, so the short name and the spelled-out chain agree across the whole domain -- including where they fail. Naming the verb in the error is the part worth having: an over-reach points at the expression you actually wrote instead of at a nested car several levels down. Two neighbouring jobs get mistaken for this one. For a depth that is not fixed, list-ref (for an element) or list-tail (for a tail) takes the number instead of spelling it in letters. For a path that mixes both, write the 3 steps out as car and cdr -- they raise in exactly the same places, so the choice is about reading, not about safety."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))" :note "(5 6) -- take the second item, then drop the first 1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(equal? (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))) (cdr (car (cdr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))))))" :note "#t -- on a WELL-SHAPED input cdadr agrees exactly with its R7RS expansion (cdr (car (cdr x))). The agreement is what the name promises; the next example is where it breaks.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(display (list 'legal (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n               'on-empty (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (cdadr '()))\n               'r7rs-expansion (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (cdr (car (cdr '()))))))\n(newline)" :note "(legal (5 6) on-empty raised r7rs-expansion raised) -- the boundary, run three ways. The legal input answers (5 6). The empty list RAISES, and the spelled-out expansion beside it raises too. The short name and the chain agree on the failure as well as on the answer, so nothing extra is needed to make cdadr stop.")
  )
  :caveats ("Read the letters right to left after dropping c and r: dad means cdr then car then cdr." "cdadr answers a LIST (a tail), because its leftmost letter is d." "(cdadr '()) raises, and so does any input too shallow along this path. That is what R7RS-small 6.4.1 asks for: it defines cdadr as (cdr (car (cdr pair))), and the chain raises at the first step that is not a pair. The error names cdadr rather than a nested car or cdr." "The argument must be nested at least 3 deep along this exact path; a flat list of the right length is NOT enough." "An empty result is a legitimate answer here, not a failure -- it means the input had exactly enough to drop and nothing after.")
  :usecases ("Dropping the label from the second row and keeping its data." "Naming one fixed path into a table stored as a list of rows so the call site reads as a coordinate." "Replacing 3 hand-written car/cdr calls with one name.")
  :related ("caadr" "cdddr" "cdaar" "cadr" "car" "cdr")
  :learn (:concept "A composed accessor: 3 car/cdr steps fused into one name, decoded right to left." :prerequisites ("car" "cdr") :progression "Learn car and cdr, then write cdadr out by hand as (cdr (car (cdr x))), then use the short name -- and keep the guard you would have written anyway.")
  :provenance "R7RS-small 6.4.1; reference record added 2026-08-02, every example executed. Conformance re-verified by execution 2026-08-02: cdadr raises on a mis-shaped input, agreeing with its R7RS expansion (cdr (car (cdr pair))). An earlier version of this record flagged the opposite -- a silent non-value -- and is superseded."
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define data '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n(define (pick s) (cdadr s))\n(display (list 'path 'dad 'gives (pick data)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (agrees? x) (equal? (cdadr x) (cdr (car (cdr x)))))\n(display (agrees? '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'legal (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n               'on-empty (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (cdadr '()))\n               'r7rs-expansion (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (cdr (car (cdr '()))))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is working with a table stored as a list of rows and needs one fixed spot: dropping the label from the second row and keeping its data. Candidates: cdadr (one name, 3 steps, and the reader must decode the letters), the spelled-out chain (cdr (car (cdr x))) (identical on well-shaped input and correctly strict on everything else), or list-ref / list-tail when the depth is a number rather than a shape. For a path that is fixed at authoring time and short, cdadr is the honest verb, and it raises when the shape is wrong exactly as the chain does."
     :explanation "eng: dropping the label from the second row and keeping its data, named once at the call site. math: the same reach written as (cdr (car (cdr x))) and compared -- they agree on well-shaped input. cs: the boundary probe -- legal input, empty input, and the spelled-out expansion, with the last two both raising."
     :meta "Why cdadr and not the spelled-out chain: 3 nested calls read as machinery, one name reads as a coordinate. Why a separate guard is now optional: both spellings are strict, so a wrong shape stops the program either way.")
    (:eng "Name a fixed path once; recompute it nowhere. Here the accessor is a shape check as well as a reach, because it raises on the wrong shape."
     :math "cdadr is the composition cdr then car then cdr, and row 2 of a matrix from column 2 onward."
     :cs "A composed accessor is only as safe as its least-defined step, and this family keeps that guarantee -- it fails exactly where its first bad step would."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Take the second item, then drop the first 1 -- one fixed route into a nested arrangement, taken in 3 steps."
     :rungs ((:domain "list" :code "(cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))")
             (:domain "the second line of a table with its row-heading removed" :code "(define page '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n(display (list 'reached (cdadr page)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "row 2 of a matrix from column 2 onward" :code "(define arr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n(display (list 'index 'dad 'entry (cdadr arr) 'same (equal? (cdadr arr) (cdr (car (cdr arr))))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In list, cdadr takes the route dad. In the everyday reading it is the second line of a table with its row-heading removed -- the same route, walked through something you can point at. In mathematics it is row 2 of a matrix from column 2 onward. All three share: a nesting deep enough to have levels, a route named once, and an answer that is a LIST (a tail)."
     :meta "What transfers is the ROUTE, not the data. Safety transfers with it: a page you can point at tells you when you have run off the end, and so does this accessor -- it raises rather than answering."
    )
    (:eng "A fixed route deserves a name; a variable route deserves a number and list-ref."
     :math "Indexing a nested structure is function composition, and composing partial functions narrows the domain at every step."
     :cs "Related patterns: x[0][0] chains in Python (which raise), .flatMap in JS (which does not), and Haskell's fst/snd (total, because the tuple type guarantees the shape)."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list 'matches-expansion (equal? (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))) (cdr (car (cdr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))))))\n               'answer (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (walk path x)\n  (cond ((null? path) x)\n        ((not (pair? x)) 'no-such-path)\n        ((eq? (car path) 'a) (walk (cdr path) (car x)))\n        (else (walk (cdr path) (cdr x)))))\n(display (list 'walker (walk '(d a d) '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n               'verb (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))\n               'same (equal? (walk '(d a d) '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))) (cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (walk path x)\n  (cond ((null? path) x)\n        ((not (pair? x)) 'no-such-path)\n        ((eq? (car path) 'a) (walk (cdr path) (car x)))\n        (else (walk (cdr path) (cdr x)))))\n(display (list 'verb-on-empty (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (cdadr '()))\n               'walker-on-empty (walk '(d a d) '())\n               'expansion-on-empty (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (cdr (car (cdr '()))))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "On any input nested deeply enough along the route dad, cdadr equals its R7RS expansion (cdr (car (cdr x))) and equals a hand-written path walker. Below that depth cdadr and the expansion both raise, while the hand-written walker reports 'no-such-path -- the walker is the only total one of the three, and that is a choice it makes rather than a property of the verb."
     :invariant "For all x nested at least 3 deep along dad. cdadr(x) = (cdr (car (cdr x))) = walk(dad, x). Outside that domain cdadr and the expansion both raise, while walk answers 'no-such-path"
     :meta "The pattern is a composed accessor proved against two independent spellings of the same route. What would falsify it: a disagreement with the expansion on a well-shaped input. The third program is the off-domain probe -- three spellings of one route, two of which raise and one of which was written to be total."
    )
    (:eng "State the domain with the invariant. An identity asserted more widely than it holds is worse than none."
     :math "Composition of partial functions is defined only where every step is; a looser composition is a different function wearing the same name."
     :cs "Two independent implementations of one route make a differential test, and differential tests are how silent divergences surface."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (walk path x)\n  (cond ((null? path) x)\n        ((not (pair? x)) 'no-such-path)\n        ((eq? (car path) 'a) (walk (cdr path) (car x)))\n        (else (walk (cdr path) (cdr x)))))\n(define (safe-cdadr x) (walk '(d a d) x))\n(display (list 'legal (safe-cdadr '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9))) 'broken (safe-cdadr '())))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (walk path x)\n  (cond ((null? path) x)\n        ((not (pair? x)) 'no-such-path)\n        ((eq? (car path) 'a) (walk (cdr path) (car x)))\n        (else (walk (cdr path) (cdr x)))))\n(define (route-agrees? path x verb) (equal? (walk path x) (verb x)))\n(display (route-agrees? '(d a d) '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)) cdadr))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (walk path x)\n  (cond ((null? path) x)\n        ((not (pair? x)) 'no-such-path)\n        ((eq? (car path) 'a) (walk (cdr path) (car x)))\n        (else (walk (cdr path) (cdr x)))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (walk p '((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)))) (list '(d a d) '(a) '(d))))\n(newline)"
     :base "cdadr walks the fixed route dad and answers a LIST (a tail)."
     :emergent "eng: a TOTAL version of cdadr -- the same route, taken one step at a time, with a named sentinel where the shape runs out. math: the route as data, checked against the verb, which is the differential test written as a function. cs: one walker serves the whole family, because the route is an argument rather than part of the name."
     :emergent-name "the path walker"
     :meta "The composition is car and cdr plus an explicit pair? test plus the route as a list. What emerges is the accessor-as-data family: cdadr is one frozen instance of walk, and there are twenty-eight of them in R7RS-small. Freezing the route buys brevity and keeps the error, because the frozen instance raises where the walk would have run out of shape."
    )
    (:eng "When you want a reach that ANSWERS rather than raises, wrap it once with a sentinel rather than guarding at every call site."
     :math "Turning an index into an argument turns twenty-eight functions into one function of two arguments."
     :cs "A raise and a sentinel you chose are both honest; a value you cannot print is not. 'no-such-path prints, compares and lets the caller carry on."))
)

