  :explanation "One of the twenty-eight composed accessors of R7RS-small 6.4.1. Decode the name by dropping the leading c and the trailing r, then reading the 4 letters RIGHT TO LEFT: adda gives car then cdr then cdr then car -- take the first item, take the third item. Written out it is (car (cdr (cdr (car pair)))). The leading letter a tells you the answer is an ELEMENT: a name whose leftmost letter is a answers an element, a name whose leftmost letter is d answers the rest of a list. You meet it on records with a three-field prefix, where the job is reading the third field of the first record. On a mis-shaped input caddar RAISES, and the message names caddar itself: (caddar '()) reports that it expected a pair or a non-empty list and got the empty list. R7RS-small 6.4.1 defines it as (car (cdr (cdr (car pair)))), and that expansion raises in the same places, so the short name and the spelled-out chain agree across the whole domain -- including where they fail. Naming the verb in the error is the part worth having: an over-reach points at the expression you actually wrote instead of at a nested car several levels down. Two neighbouring jobs get mistaken for this one. For a depth that is not fixed, list-ref (for an element) or list-tail (for a tail) takes the number instead of spelling it in letters. For a path that mixes both, write the 4 steps out as car and cdr -- they raise in exactly the same places, so the choice is about reading, not about safety."
